Section IV: Step-by-Step Prototype Fabrication & Assembly Guide
1. Cleanroom & Sterilization Requirements
 * Assembly Environment: All fluidic, cellular, and membrane assemblies must be constructed within an ISO Class 7 cleanroom or a sterile laminar flow hood.
 * Component Sterilization: PEEK housing components undergo ethylene oxide (EtO) sterilization; silicon nanopore cassettes undergo gamma irradiation (25\text{ kGy}); electronics receive 70\% isopropyl alcohol surface disinfection.
2. Assembly Sequence
Step 1: Enclosure & Thermal Plate Installation
 * Mill the main housing from medical-grade PEEK stock. Integrate internal structural ribbing to maximize crush resistance.
 * Press-fit dual fluoroelastomer (FKM) O-rings into perimeter grooves around the lower chassis tray.
 * Mount a low-profile thermoelectric Peltier junction to the rear wall, applying thermally conductive adhesive. Connect micro-thermocouple leads to the analog sensor header.
Step 2: Hemofilter Cassette & Acoustic Transducer Mounting
 * Verify the 5 to 7 nm silicon nanopore grid under 100\times optical inspection. Confirm covalent grafting of the sub-5 nm zwitterionic pSBMA hydrogel monolayer.
 * Epoxy-bond thin-film Aluminum Nitride (AlN) piezoceramic ring transducers directly to the outer titanium cassette housing.
 * Secure the SNM array into the titanium cassette using FKM compression gaskets. Torque titanium assembly screws to 0.8\text{ Nm} in a cross-star pattern.
Step 3: PEC Reactor & Gas Venting Integration
 * Install the nanostructured hematite / NiOOH photoanode inside the quartz microfluidic flow cell.
 * Position the 460 nm blue micro-LED array 2.0\text{ mm} above the photoanode window, delivering 25\text{ mW/cm}^2 continuous irradiance.
 * Install the 0.2\ \mu\text{m} hydrophobic expanded PTFE degassing membrane over the exhaust manifold to permit passive venting of evolved nitrogen and carbon dioxide gases.
Step 4: Fluidic Plumbing & Pump Drive
 * Connect platinum-cured silicone tubing (3.2\text{ mm} inner diameter) between the fluid inlet, SNM hemofilter, PEC cell, and outlet manifolds.
 * Install dual opposite-phase piezoceramic diaphragm pumps. Wire drivers to run in opposite-phase modulation (30\text{ to }60\text{ mL/min}) to minimize shear stress.
 * Attach PVDF quick-disconnect couplings to external port mounts.
Step 5: Electronics Mounting & Firmware Flashing
 * Secure the 4-layer RISC-V controller PCB into the upper isolated dry compartment.
 * Wire piezoceramic drivers, ISFET telemetry sensors (K^+, pH, temperature), and LED power rails to the controller header.
 * Flash the bare-metal ce_os-kernel Rust binary (riscv32imac-unknown-none-elf) via the SWD debugging interface.
Section V: Clinical Operational Playbook & Safety Protocols
Phase 1: Pre-Flight Priming & System Verification
 * Flush the fluid circuit with sterile isotonic saline to displace ambient air bubbles completely.
 * Execute the ce_os metabolic audit. Verify that the RPTEC/TERT1 cells in the tubule bioreactor are metabolically active and that the internal fluid heater has stabilized at 37°C.
 * Clean the patient application site with sterile, alcohol-free isotonic wipes. Apply the UGIRAP gecko adhesive pad to the epidermis using uniform normal pressure (\approx 15\text{ g/cm}^2) to engage van der Waals contact.
 * Verify via BLE telemetry that the embedded sensors report stable contact impedance and zero localized pressure hotspots.
Phase 2: Steady-State Wear & Active Operation
 * Fluid circulates continuously through the closed loop at 30\text{ to }60\text{ mL/min}.
 * The onboard microcontroller continuously monitors Transmembrane Pressure (TMP). If a minor TMP rise occurs (indicating early concentration polarization), the system automatically increases the duty cycle of the piezo-acoustic transducers to sweep the membrane.
 * Uremic waste passes into the closed-loop dialysate. Verify that the 460 nm blue LEDs in the PEC cell are illuminated and that hydrophobic vents remain unobstructed to permit gas release.
 * Recommended wear duration before routine site inspection and patch rotation: 72 continuous hours.
Phase 3: Troubleshooting Matrix
| Observed Fault | Primary Sensor Indicator | Failsafe Action / Corrective Protocol |
|---|---|---|
| Acoustic Defouling Degradation | Gradual TMP Spike (> 18\text{ mmHg}) | Automatically trigger the Magnetic Micro-Wiper via external actuator to physically scrape the SNM. |
| Micro-Gas Bubble Ingress | Optical Refractive Sensor Alert | CE-OS Firewall actuates electromagnetic isolation valve within 9.15 ns, routing fluid to degassing loop. |
| High Interstitial Potassium | ISFET Sensor (K^+ > 6.2\text{ mmol/L}) | Throttles recirculation pump, alerts user via BLE telemetry, and increases sorbent bed flux. |
| Firmware Logic Anomaly | Watchdog Timer Alert | Firewall forces hardware reset, shifting system safely into Passive Synthetic Bypass Mode. |
